            "content": "Initially, Carter has 20 green and 20 red M&Ms. After eating 12 green M&Ms, he has 8 green M&Ms left. His sister eats half the red M&Ms, so she eats 10 red M&Ms, leaving 10 red M&Ms. She then adds 14 yellow M&Ms. Now, the jar contains 8 green, 10 red, and 14 yellow M&Ms, totaling 32 M&Ms.\n\nThe probability of picking a green M&M is the number of green M&Ms divided by the total number of M&Ms, which is \\( \\frac{8}{32} = \\frac{1}{4} \\). To convert this to a percentage, we multiply by 100, giving \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\times 100 = 25\\% \\).\n\nFINAL: 25",
            "role": "assistant"
